WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.410
- I took a little diversion

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.240
on the way back from
filming last week's video.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.490
It is very cold and
windy at this altitude.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.650
But, tucked away in the Swiss Alps,

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.660
there's a military airfield with not one

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.810
but two public roads that go
straight across the runway

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.790
and I have wanted to see
this for a long time.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.620
This is Meiringen air base.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.240
And not only do the fighter jets here

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.850
have to cope with civilian
traffic crossing their runway

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.221
but the spectacle of military aircraft
roaring down the tarmac

00:00:24.221 --> 00:00:26.220
attracts tourists 
from all over the world.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.830
So I wanted to ask: how
do they operate an airbase

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.780
where the public aren't
just turning up to watch

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.530
but they can also walk, ride,
or drive straight through?

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.360
- I don't know how many times
I took off,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.650
how many times I've landed.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.930
Well, let's say around a hundred takeoffs,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.940
which equals a hundred landings.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.950
But every single one is very cool.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.411
At times, there can be
a lot of tourists and observers 

00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:49.020
all around the airfield,
especially in summer.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.080
They stay behind the barriers.
They're outside of the fence.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.520
But it's all threat-level dependent.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.220
So at the moment we're at peace,
there's no problem.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.770
If we were to find ourselves
in a crisis...

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.920
it would be
another story.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:02.513
- Okay.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.330
All right. Where am I
allowed to stand?

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.340
- Alright, look down. Right here.
You're going to be all safe.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.173
- Yep.

00:01:09.173 --> 00:01:11.280
- The jets are going
to come from that side.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.075
Going to take off here.
Back here, you're safe.

00:01:13.075 --> 00:01:17.353
- [exhales, laughs]

00:01:17.353 --> 00:01:18.750
I mean you fly them, so...!

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.583
- I do!

00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:21.000
[crossing warning beeps]

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.200
When we're ready for takeoff

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.300
the air traffic controllers
close the barriers.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.796
They themselves have a look if
they see anything suspicious.

00:01:26.796 --> 00:01:29.580
And then whenever the
barriers get closed,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.050
there's guys from the military police

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.610
on both sides of the barriers.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.680
Also, there's the runway controller

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.320
who observes every takeoff
and every landing.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.790
He could step in as well.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.370
So we as pilots, we can't
do much ourselves.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.223
- There are a few other
runways in the world

00:01:43.223 --> 00:01:44.602
with roads across them,
but they're all

00:01:44.602 --> 00:01:46.809
very small and very remote.

00:01:46.809 --> 00:01:50.220
In Sumburgh, on the island of Shetland,
in the far north of the UK

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.440
there is a commercial airport
with a similar setup

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.070
across one of its two runways,.
But that is very remote.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.883
It used to be that the only road
in and out of Gibraltar

00:01:58.883 --> 00:02:01.140
went right across a
massive airport runway.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.450
One that took full size passenger jets.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.250
But there is finally
a new tunnel there now

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.240
after years of delays.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.550
So cars and trucks no longer go that way.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.400
So this runway here in
Switzerland would be just

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.110
a little side note, if it wasn't

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.390
for the tourist industry
that sprung up around it.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.520
If you're the sort of
aviation or military nerd

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.160
that wants to see fighter jets
up close and personal

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.470
this is probably on your
list of places to visit.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.356
And if you didn't know
about it before this video,

00:02:24.356 --> 00:02:25.950
it probably is now.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.870
There's a very nice
restaurant just off the base.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.060
One of the hangers is open
to the public occasionally

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.270
and there's a massive military
air show every October.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.570
This place is good PR
for the Swiss Air Force.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.810
- If ever there should be
anyone between the barriers

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.220
when we are coming back to land,

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.050
we would be ordered
to go-around.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.750
So we would abort our approach,
climb again and

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:49.500
do a second approach whenever
the issue has been resolved.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.960
When we take off, it's basically
the other way around.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.750
We taxi it towards the runway
and once we report ready

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.600
to the air traffic controller,
they close the barriers

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.610
and they give us our
clearance to to take off

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.320
once everything is settled

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.180
- It's not like there are
any statistics to compare

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.190
but that crossing is probably more safe

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.020
than most railroad crossings.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.000
Although to be fair, the rail industry
is trying to get rid

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.626
as many of those as they can,
because they are an

00:03:10.626 --> 00:03:12.080
unnecessary safety risk.
[loud jet engine, increasing] 

00:03:22.865 --> 00:03:25.948
[jet engine roaring]

00:03:44.992 --> 00:03:48.240
I cannot explain how loud that was.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.270
I've got full ear-defender earplugs in.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.970
I think it might have
broken my microphone.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:57.000
Wow. We had one take to get that.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.093
I hope I didn't fluff my lines. [laughing]

